  "hardini-story-data.js": {
    "A Boy in Hardin": 1,
    "In 1808, a boy named Youssef Kassab was born in Hardin, a mountain village in Lebanon. His family belonged to the Maronite Church, and prayer was part of the life around him. Four of his brothers would become priests or monks. Youssef did not know all the turns his own path would take. He saw men who lived, worked, and prayed together, and he began to wonder whether God was calling him to that kind of life. Imagine looking from a hillside toward a distant monastery. That is a picture to help us enter his world, not a record of one particular afternoon. A calling can begin quietly, before anyone else knows what it will become.": 1,
    "An original retelling of the Church biography; imaginative scenes are identified as composites, not recorded events.": 9,
    "A New Name in Qozhaya": 1,
    "When he was a young man, Youssef entered the Lebanese Maronite Order. In 1828 he came to the monastery of Saint Anthony in Qozhaya, near the Qadisha Valley. He later received the name Nimatullah, meaning \"grace of God.\" A new name did not make him a different person overnight. His days were filled with prayer and shared work. He also learned to bind books, putting pages together so they could be read and kept. Picture careful hands folding and joining a book. We do not know the exact book he held on any one day, but the work itself belongs to his story. Prayer did not replace useful work. He learned to give his attention to both.": 1,
    "Learning at Kfifan": 1,
    "In 1830 Nimatullah made his promise to live as a monk. He went to the monastery of Saints Cyprian and Justina in Kfifan, where he studied philosophy and theology. Studying meant asking difficult questions, reading, remembering, and listening to other people. In 1833 he was ordained a priest. Soon he taught younger monks and helped guide their studies. A teacher cannot learn for a student, but can give that student time and care. What he taught was not only written on pages. Other monks could notice how he prayed, how he worked, and how he treated the person beside him. Being learned did not mean he was finished learning how to love.": 1,
    "A Student Named Charbel": 1,
    "One of the younger monks formed in Nimatullah's school was Charbel Makhlouf. The Church later remembered both men as saints. We do not have to invent a conversation between them to see why this mattered. A teacher's patient work may travel farther than he can imagine through the lives of his students. Picture the kind of classroom they would have known: a text to study, questions to ask, and a teacher who takes those questions seriously. This is an imaginative picture, not a record of a particular lesson. Charbel would one day live as a hermit and become known far beyond Lebanon. Nimatullah's own road was different. He stayed close to the shared life of the monastery, teaching and caring for his brother monks.": 1,
    "Beside the Tabernacle": 1,
    "The Church's account says that Nimatullah spent long periods praying before the Blessed Sacrament. For Catholics, the Eucharist is a precious way Jesus is present to his people. Nimatullah did not see prayer as a prize earned by working hard. He returned to the chapel because he wanted to be near God. Sometimes he gave up sleep to pray, but children do not need to copy that. Rest and care for your body matter. You can pray in a small, honest way, even for a minute. After prayer, Nimatullah still had people to meet and work to do. The same life held silence in the chapel and ordinary voices at the table.": 1,
    "Staying with Others": 1,
    "Lebanon went through frightening civil conflict in the 1840s. Nimatullah suffered with his people. His brother, Father Elisha, suggested he leave community life for a hermitage, a place where a monk lives more alone. Nimatullah chose to stay among the other monks. This was his own calling, not a rule for everyone. Some people serve God in quiet places, and some stay close to a busy community. Nimatullah believed that living with others gave him daily chances to practice kindness. He did not have to solve the country's troubles by himself. He could welcome a brother, teach a student, and try not to add to another person's hurt. When times were hard, those were real things to do.": 1,
    "The Quiet Kind of Leadership": 1,
    "In 1845 Nimatullah was appointed Assistant General of his order. He helped guide monks and asked that some of them be sent for further study. Leadership might have made him seem important, but he did not want special treatment. The Church biography says he declined a servant who would have attended to him because of his office. He also turned down the highest office in the order. We cannot know every thought behind these choices. We can see a man who wanted to serve more than he wanted to be noticed. The account says he could be hard on himself yet gentle with his brothers. That is not a lesson to be hard on yourself. It is a reminder that people need patience, including the person doing the helping.": 1,
    "A Life Shared": 1,
    "The monks knew Nimatullah through many kinds of days: lessons, prayer, meetings, and work. He cared deeply for Mary, the mother of Jesus, and often prayed to her. Pope John Paul II later spoke of the way Nimatullah welcomed his brothers and offered comfort. A kind word does not make every sorrow disappear, but it can tell someone they do not face it alone. A monastery is not full of perfect people. It is a place where real people must keep learning to live together. Nimatullah's story is not about one dazzling moment that made him holy. It is about returning, day after day, to God and to the people beside him. That steady return took time.": 1,
    "Remembering Him": 1,
    "In December 1858 Nimatullah became gravely ill at Kfifan. He died on December 14, at the age of fifty. The Church's account says he held an image of Mary near the end of his life. The monks and people who had known him remembered his prayer and his care. Many years later, the Church studied his life. Pope John Paul II beatified him in 1998 and proclaimed him a saint in 2004. His story is not a promise that prayer will stop illness, and no child should avoid care to be like him. When you are sick or frightened, tell a trusted adult and accept help. Nimatullah had been a teacher, a monk, and a neighbor. What stayed with others was the love he practiced in those roles.": 1,
    "Points of reflection": 1,
    "First, Youssef listened for his own calling before becoming Brother Nimatullah. God gives each person a life to live with love. What good work makes you curious, and which trusted person can help you explore it?\n\nSecond, Nimatullah learned to bind books and later taught young monks, including Charbel. Jesus taught his friends to serve one another. What could you learn patiently, then share without showing off?\n\nThird, he chose to stay with his community through hard times. God's teaching about loving our neighbors reaches the people closest to us. Is there someone near you who needs a kind word or a listening ear? If someone is unsafe, tell a trusted adult.\n\nFourth, he prayed before the Eucharist and then returned to the people around him. Prayer and care belong together; neither asks you to ignore your own needs. What short prayer and what helpful step could you try this week?\n\nChoose one step. Ask God to help you take it gently, and remember that you may ask others for help too.": 1,
    "An original reflection joining Nimatullah’s documented life to Jesus’ teaching of neighbor-love; not a recorded speech.": 1
  },
